{
  "dom_id": "step-q183736",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ compute $P(Z>2.31).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-2.5\\,$ | $\\,0.0062\\,$ | $\\,0.0060\\,$ | $\\,0.0059\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0082\\,$ | $\\,0.0080\\,$ | $\\,0.0078\\,$ |\n| $\\,-2.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0107\\,$ | $\\,0.0104\\,$ | $\\,0.0102\\,$ |",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/f25b8fea7403579d1ee40cc00b9447e3362dc28c2e9d41f762f51df5e11daaf8.png)\n\nFor a random variable $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ we have the following symmetry property for $a>0:$\n\n$$\nP(Z>a)=P(Z<-a)=\u03a6(-a)\n$$\n\nWe're given that $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ and we wish to compute $P(Z>2.31).$ The required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/a3e01ca4fd737cea9afe95374a17c0428f83a6717d98a7557222a856aa2d0c02.png)\n\nBy symmetry, this equals the area corresponding to the probability $P(Z<-2.31).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069722/assets/ce206061194e357b9cd75f9b260e4035a8600726a17cc0ee04ab39219bd45e94.png)\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(Z>2.31)=P(Z<-2.31)=\u03a6(-2.31)=0.0104.\n$$",
